# Assembly theory, the measure of how much history an object carries. The assembly index of an object is the least number
# of joining steps needed to build it from basic parts, where any sub-assembly already made can be reused. Every assembly
# pathway is a binary tree: the leaves are the basic parts and each internal node is a join of two sub-assemblies. This
# file MODELS an assembly as such a tree and DEFINES the number of parts, the leaves, and the number of joins, the
# internal nodes. It PROVES the invariant that governs every assembly: the number of parts is exactly one more than the
# number of joins, so building an object from p parts always costs p minus one joins, no matter the shape of the pathway.
# This is the assembly counterpart of Euler's relation for trees, proved by structural induction over the actual tree of
# joins. It records that a balanced run of eight parts has doubling index three while a no-reuse chain costs seven, so
# reuse beats the naive path.

# An assembly pathway: either a single basic part, or a join of two sub-assemblies. Every assembly is such a binary tree,
# the leaves the basic parts and the internal nodes the joins.
form build
  case part
  case join
    link left, like build
    link right, like build

# Reflecting an assembly pathway: a part stays a part, and a join swaps its two reflected sub-assemblies. Since a join is
# the union of two independent sub-assemblies, the order of the two branches does not change what is built, so reflection
# is a symmetry of the pathway.
task reflect
  take b, like build
  like build
  fork case, read b
    case part
      send back
        make part
    case join
      send back
        make join
          bind left
            call reflect
              read right
          bind right
            call reflect
              read left

# REFLECTION IS AN INVOLUTION: reflecting an assembly twice returns the original pathway, reflect (reflect b) = b, for
# every assembly b. So the two branches of every join are interchangeable, the order in which sub-assemblies are combined
# does not affect the object built, which is why the assembly index counts unordered joins. Proved by structural induction
# over the assembly tree, using the induction hypothesis on both subtrees.
rule reflection-is-an-involution
  seat b, like build
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect
        call reflect
          read b
      read b
  fold b

# A BALANCED RUN OF EIGHT parts is three doublings, since eight is two to the third: 8 = 2 * 2 * 2. The reuse of each
# doubled run is what makes the assembly index the logarithm rather than the length.
hold a-run-of-eight-is-three-doublings
  call is-equal
    code 8
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 2

# REUSE BEATS THE NAIVE PATH: the doubling index three is below the no-reuse chain cost seven for the same length eight,
# so a structured object is cheaper to assemble than a random one, the compression assembly theory measures, 3 below 7.
hold reuse-beats-the-naive-path
  call is-below
    code 3
    code 7
